nodecode-windows-tasks is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 9778252252aeec293e0d234ffe24d20de9eb2fc4
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's windows-tasks folder as a Nodecode cell: its two
read-only tools, the same data, the same answers. What it keeps: the two
powershell.exe scripts the plugin ran, character for character apart from
their being strings here -- the error action preference and UTF-8 output
header, the one-call listing that collects Get-ScheduledTaskInfo into a table
keyed by path and name so a task whose state cannot be read loses only its
last and next run, ConvertTo-Json with a depth of five and compressed, and the
per-task Export-ScheduledTask wrapped in try/catch; the argv it ran them with
(-NoProfile, -NonInteractive, -ExecutionPolicy Bypass, -Command) and the
deadline that kills a call which outlives it; the reading of the answer -- a
BOM stripped, one object read as a one-row listing, rows of a type that is not
an object dropped -- and of the exported definition, whose XML declaration is
skipped and whose trigger classes, schedules, repetition, actions, principal
and settings are read into the same words: 'at logon of ...', 'every 1 day(s)
at 03:00, repeating every PT1H for PT4H', 'the action as the command line a
person would run'. The result-code table and the trigger-class table are the
plugin's own, meaning for meaning, as are the filters (a path substring, a
state, failed-only with a task that never ran not a failure, Windows' own tasks
left out unless asked for, a limit bounded at 500), the notes that say what a
filter left out, and the escape of the one value interpolated into PowerShell (a
single quote doubled inside a single-quoted string). The definition's XML is
read by this cell's own small reader -- elements, text and entities -- because
the plugin's Python carried ElementTree and this image has no XML parser.

What is left out, and why: the Python package itself (pyproject.toml, the
setuptools packaging, the pytest suite) is not bundled; the cell is Common Lisp
and its tests are this repository's. The plugin's CREATE_NO_WINDOW flag has no
counterpart in SBCL's run-program, so a call started from a windowless process
may flash a console window; nothing else about the run changed. The plugin's
Python exceptions became one condition, windows-tasks-error, and its answers
stay JSON with ok true or false as they were, so a model reads the same
payload it read there.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell runs the same Windows
PowerShell cmdlets the plugin ran, on the same machine, and its own code is
Common Lisp.
